      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's influence extends far beyond music-based animation. Its innovative mix of animation styles - from rotoscoping to collage to classical character animation - opened doors for experimental animators worldwide. <a href=\"/movie/12593\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"12593\">Fritz the Cat</a> (1972) followed its adult-oriented path, while <a href=\"/movie/29204\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"29204\">American Pop</a> (1981) borrowed its technique of building narrative through popular music. The Beatles' limited involvement (voice actors replaced them except for the finale) ironically allowed the animators more creative freedom, resulting in sequences like \"When I'm Sixty-Four\" that remain masterclasses in imaginative visualization."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's technical innovations deserve special attention. The multilayered cel animation created depth that was revolutionary for its time. Sequences like \"Sea of Time\" and \"Sea of Science\" pushed the boundaries of what animation could achieve visually. These techniques influenced later works like <a href=\"/movie/11837\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11837\">Watership Down</a> (1978) and <a href=\"/movie/629990\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"629990\">The Wall</a> (1982), proving that animation could tackle sophisticated themes while maintaining artistic innovation. SUBHEAD: Legacy and Modern Animation"
      },
      {
